Let IRQ handlers run to their iret after a present-in-place quit.

Headless present-in-place sets the sticky g_frame_present_quit at the first
eligible VBlank after --frames. From then on runtime_should_yield() returns
true at every generated instruction, including inside an IRQ handler: the
quit check has no IRQ-depth guard, while the VBlank yield right below it
has one (g_irq_nest_depth == 0). An IRQ that arrives after the quit
dispatches the BIOS vector 0x18, the generated code yields at its first
instruction, and runtime_irq()'s drive loop re-dispatches it until its
watchdog gives up: "runtime_irq: handler at depth 1 did not iret after
4000000 dispatches (R15=0x00000018, ...) -- abandoning". IRQs keep arriving
after the quit only while an interpreter bridge is still running and
ticking the clock, so the number of messages is the number of IRQs between
the quit and the end of that bridge.

Fix: apply the same guard to the quit check. The handler runs to its iret,
and the mainline yields at its next generated instruction, as it does for
the VBlank yield. The quit is only ever set at IRQ depth 0, so no other
path changes.

Tested on macOS arm64 (Apple clang 21) at gbarecomp 5e89943 plus this
series: gbarecomp's ctest, 52 of 52. On a commercial game whose runs end
while a long interpreter bridge runs (headless, healing off), runs of
3,600 and 12,000 frames printed the watchdog message 80 and 772 times
without this patch, and none with it; runs of 2,700 and 5,400 frames print
none with it either. With and without the patch, the 12,000-frame run
exits 0 at the same frame, 12,385 (the bridge ends 385 frames after the
quit), and the 3,600-frame run at 3,644.

Apply it together with the call-return leak fix (0003 in this series). In
the game above, without that fix, an IRQ handler abandoned after the quit
was what ended a mis-stopped bridge: the bridge's stop PC is only reached
when that handler's code is interpreted. With this patch alone the handler
runs natively, the bridge never stops, and the 2,700-frame run aborts at
the 200M-instruction cap instead of stopping at frame 2,706.

diff --git a/src/runtime/runtime_bus_bridge.cpp b/src/runtime/runtime_bus_bridge.cpp
index 044fc13..efd5af1 100644
--- a/src/runtime/runtime_bus_bridge.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/runtime_bus_bridge.cpp
@@ -1032,7 +1032,12 @@ extern "C" bool runtime_should_yield(void) {
     }
 
     // Present-in-place quit: fully unwind the guest to the runner once requested.
-    if (g_frame_present_quit) return true;
+    // Not while an IRQ handler is on the host stack: runtime_irq() drives it to
+    // its iret, so a yield at every instruction would spin its loop to the
+    // 4,000,000-dispatch watchdog and abandon the interrupt. The handler runs
+    // to its iret, and the mainline yields at its next instruction instead.
+    // Same guard as the VBlank yield below.
+    if (g_frame_present_quit && g_irq_nest_depth == 0u) return true;
 
     bool halted = bus && bus->io().halted();
 
